    <item>
      <title>The different brackets in PowerShell</title>
      <link>https://diecknet.de/en/2023/11/28/powershell-brackets/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 28 Nov 2023 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <author>Andreas Dieckmann</author>
      <guid>https://diecknet.de/en/2023/11/28/powershell-brackets/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;When is which bracket the right one in PowerShell?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#fff;background-color:#1f1f24;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-powershell&#34; data-lang=&#34;powershell&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#6c7986&#34;&gt;# Different brackets in PowerShell&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;() {} []&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The brackets have different purposes. By the way, I would not count the greater-than and less-than signs &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; as brackets. For all real brackets, if you open one, you must close it again.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;round-brackets--&#34;&gt;Round brackets &lt;code&gt;( )&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Round brackets are used in PowerShell to group an expression or command, or to enclose the parameters for .NET methods.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
